\par In short: Within Tolkien's gene
ral system there is room for many extra complications here, if one wants to draw the full consequences of the underlying phonological evolution that he imagined. The augment, the prefixed stem-vowel occurring in the perfect tense, does seem to have been p
refixed so early that the following consonants still had not assumed the shape initial consonants would have in Quenya. (In WJ:366, Tolkien cites some "prehistoric" perfects already featuring the augment.) To return to our first example, the }{
\i\lang1033 d}{\lang1033 - of the stem }{\i\lang1033 DA(N)T}{\lang1033 - "fall" had not yet become }{\b\lang1033 l}{\lang1033 - as in }{\b\lang1033 lanta}{\lang1033 , and then the perfect "has fallen" could plausibly be (}{\b\lang1033 azanti\'eb}{
\lang1033  >) }{\b\lang1033 aranti\'eb}{\lang1033  because original }{\i\lang1033 d}{\lang1033  became }{\b\lang1033 z}{\lang1033  and then }{\b\lang1033 r}{\lang1033 
 following a vowel. So should we get in touch with Enya and tell her that she must record her song all over again, with }{\b\lang1033 morni\'eb aranti\'eb}{\lang1033  instead of }{\b\lang1033 alanti\'eb}{\lang1033 ? 
\par We cannot tell. In this and all the other phonological cases just listed, it is simply impossible to predict to what extent Tolkien meant the "historically justified" forms to have succumbed to analogical leveling \endash 
 if such leveling occurred at all in augmented verbs. Indeed, at present we cannot even know whether Tolkien ever considered these things. Given the history Tolkien imagined for Quenya \endash  that in Middle-earth it was nobody's mother-tongu
e, but merely an ancient ritual language \endash  it seems quite plausible that the grammar of Exilic Quenya would tend to become somewhat simplified. }{\b\lang1033 Lanta}{\lang1033 - as a verb corresponds to the noun }{\b\lang1033 lanta}{\lang1033 
, compounded in }{\b\lang1033 lasselanta}{\lang1033  "leaf-fall, autumn" (this word occurs in Appendix D of LotR, making this form about as canonical as any Quenya word can be). If the compound was old enough, this "should" have been **}{\b\lang1033 
lasseranta}{\lang1033  instead, since the original }{\i\lang1033 d}{\lang1033  of }{\i\lang1033 dant}{\lang1033 - is here intervocalic. 
\par Another possibility that we considered above was that verbs derived from original stems in }{\i\lang1033 mb}{\lang1033 -, }{\i\lang1033 nd}{\lang1033 - and }{\i\lang1033 \'f1g}{\lang1033 
- would still preserve these combinations following the perfect augment, so that (say) }{\b\lang1033 mar}{\lang1033 - "dwell" from the stem }{\i\lang1033 MBAR}{\lang1033  should have the perfect form }{\b\lang1033 amb\'e1ri\'eb}{\lang1033  instead of }{
\b\lang1033 am\'e1ri\'eb}{\lang1033 . It may be noted that in the }{\i\lang1033 Etymologies}{\lang1033 , Tolkien derived the Quenya word for elephant \endash  }{\b\lang1033 andamunda}{\lang1033  \endash  from primitive }{\i\lang1033 andambund\'e2}{
\lang1033  "long-snouted" (see entry }{\i\lang1033 MBUD}{\lang1033 ). This Quenya form could just as well have been **}{\b\lang1033 andambunda}{\lang1033 , for original }{\i\lang1033 mb}{\lang1033  could well survive in this position
. Yet the second element "-snouted" may here seem to have been altered from -}{\b\lang1033 mbunda}{\lang1033  to -}{\b\lang1033 munda}{\lang1033  by analogy with such forms as }{\b\lang1033 mundo}{\lang1033  "nose" (itself descended from primitive }{
\i\lang1033 mbundu}{\lang1033 , initial }{\i\lang1033 mb}{\lang1033 - regularly producing Quenya }{\b\lang1033 m}{\lang1033 -). Of course, such compounds may not 
tell us how augmented perfect forms would behave; the latter would probably be perceived as unitary words, whereas compounds are more obviously a combination of two elements that can usually appear independently as well. Yet as long as there is no definit
e
 evidence to the contrary, writers may choose to assume that even in the perfect tense, the initial consonants of verbs were normally re-formed by analogy with the unaugmented shape of the verb. We don't have to make our Neo-Quenya more complicated than w
e definitely know Tolkien imagined his Quenya to be. 
