\par If we start considering how the underlying phonological evolution could affect inflected forms in Quenya, yet another possible complication has to do with nouns in -}{\b\lang1033 il}{\lang1033  that is descended from primitive Elvish -}{\i\lang1033 la}{
\lang1033 . Tolkien's idea was that a primitive word like }{\i\lang1033 makla}{\lang1033  "sword" first became }{\i\lang1033 makl}{\lang1033 , since final short -}{\i\lang1033 a}{\lang1033  disappeared quite early. This }{\i\lang1033 makl}{\lang1033 
 was pronounced as two syllables, }{\i\lang1033 mak-l}{\lang1033  with a syllabic L at the end (just like English }{\i\lang1033 little}{\lang1033  is pronounced }{\i\lang1033 lit-l}{\lang1033 ). Eventually, a new vowel }{\i\lang1033 i}{\lang1033 
 developed before this syllabic L, so that the word came to end in -}{\b\lang1033 il}{\lang1033 . In this way, an original }{\i\lang1033 makla}{\lang1033  evolved into Quenya }{\b\lang1033 macil}{\lang1033 . Other words of this kind include }{\b\lang1033 
tecil}{\lang1033  "pen" and }{\b\lang1033 hecil}{\lang1033  "waif, outcast" (primitive }{\i\lang1033 tekla}{\lang1033 , }{\i\lang1033 hekla}{\lang1033 ).
\par \tab But what happens if such words are to receive case endings? If, for instance, we are to add the instrumental ending -}{\b\lang1033 nen}{\lang1033  to }{\b\lang1033 macil}{\lang1033  to express "with a sword", should we start from the primitive form }
{\i\lang1033 makla-nen}{\lang1033 ? This combination would develop differently. In }{\i\lang1033 makla-nen}{\lang1033  the final -}{\i\lang1033 a}{\lang1033  of }{\i\lang1033 makla}{\lang1033  is not final at all, and th
en it would presumably not be lost. One could argue that in Quenya, the historically justified instrumental form "with a sword" should be something like }{\b\lang1033 malcanen}{\lang1033  \endash  since in Quenya the cluster }{\i\lang1033 kl}{\lang1033 
 occurring between vowels turned into }{\i\lang1033 lk}{\lang1033  = }{\b\lang1033 lc}{\lang1033 . (For instance, }{\b\lang1033 alcar}{\lang1033  "glory" comes from primitive }{\i\lang1033 aklar-}{\lang1033 ; compare Sindarin }{\i\lang1033 aglar}{
\lang1033 .) Similarly, "with a pen" (}{\b\lang1033 tecil}{\lang1033 ) could be }{\b\lang1033 telcanen}{\lang1033 , since }{\b\lang1033 tecil}{\lang1033  descends from primitive }{\i\lang1033 tekla}{\lang1033 .
\par \tab When constructing case forms, is it permissible to take the simpler route of simply starting from }{\b\lang1033 macil}{\lang1033 , }{\b\lang1033 tecil}{\lang1033  etc. and inflecting them like other nouns? (Then the instrumental form of }{
\b\lang1033 macil}{\lang1033  would be either }{\b\lang1033 macilenen}{\lang1033  with a connecting vowel before -}{\b\lang1033 nen}{\lang1033 , or perhaps }{\b\lang1033 macil}{\b\ul\lang1033 a}{\b\lang1033 nen}{\lang1033 
 with the otherwise lost final vowel of the old ending -}{\i\lang1033 la}{\lang1033  preserved, or even }{\b\lang1033 macilden}{\lang1033  for }{\i\lang1033 macilnen}{\lang1033  with the normal development }{\i\lang1033 ln}{\lang1033  > }{\b\lang1033 ld}{
\lang1033 .) It may be noted that after deriving the Quenya noun }{\b\lang1033 hecil}{\lang1033  "outcast, waif" from primitive }{\i\lang1033 hekla}{\lang1033 , Tolkien also mentioned masculine and feminine forms of it: masc. }{\b\lang1033 hecilo}{
\lang1033 , fem. }{\b\lang1033 hecil\'eb}{\lang1033  (WJ:365). These words seem to be derived simply by adding the masculine and feminine endings -}{\b\lang1033 o}{\lang1033 , -}{\b\lang1033 \'eb}{\lang1033  to the word }{\b\lang1033 hecil}{\lang1033 
 as it exists in the "contemporary" form of Quenya: The masculine ending -}{\b\lang1033 o}{\lang1033  probably descends from primitive -}{\i\lang1033 \'f4}{\lang1033 , and primitive }{\i\lang1033 hekla-\'f4}{\lang1033  might come out as **}{\b\lang1033 
helco}{\lang1033  in Quenya \endash  but this form does not occur. If derivational endings can be added to the noun }{\b\lang1033 hecil}{\lang1033 
 with no further complications, then it is perhaps also permissible to attach case endings to these nouns in -}{\b\lang1033 il}{\lang1033  with no strange things happening to t
he noun-stem itself. Again, it may not be necessary to take into account the entire historical development that is supposed to have produced the "contemporary" (}{\i\lang1033 synchronic}{\lang1033 , as linguists might say) form of the noun.
\par 
\par As I have already indicated, there is currently no definite answer available to many of the questions raised above. We have merely sketched some of the }{\i\lang1033 potential}{\lang1033 
 implications of Tolkien's general phonological system, which is inseparable from his vision of how Quenya had evolved from the earlier
 forms of Elvish. As we have seen, there are hints that analogical leveling would sometimes be permissible, but also examples that point in the opposite direction. Only future publications can throw more light on these problems, assuming that Tolkien disc
usses such matters in his manuscripts. There will certainly be some clues, given Tolkien's deep interest in the historical evolution of his languages (which to him often seems to be more important than their "modern" or synchronic manifestation).
\par 
\par }{\cf2\lang1033 STEM VARIATION
\par }{\lang1033 In Lesson Three of this course, one important fact about Quenya was introduced: Compared to the endingless form (the }{\i\lang1033 simplex}{\lang1033  form) of a word, the word will in some cases subtly }{\i\lang1033 change}{\lang1033 
 when endings are added to it \endash  as when the plural form of }{\b\lang1033 talan}{\lang1033  "floor" is }{\b\lang1033 talami}{\lang1033  rather than **}{\b\lang1033 talani}{\lang1033 . Thus the simplex }{\b\lang1033 talan}{\lang1033  has the }{
\i\lang1033 stem-form}{\lang1033  }{\b\lang1033 talam}{\lang1033 -. Typically, the stem-form reflects how the word appeared at older stages of the language (for instance, the simplex }{\i\lang1033 talam}{\lang1033  was altered to }{\b\lang1033 talan}{
\lang1033  because Quenya at one stage no longer tolerated final -}{\i\lang1033 m}{\lang1033  and changed it to the closest permissible sound, namely -}{\b\lang1033 n}{\lang1033 
). I wrote, reassuringly: "The student should not despair, thinking that all sorts of strange things typically happen whenever you add an ending to a Quenya word, so th
at there is a great potential for making embarrassing mistakes (or at least very much extra stuff to memorize). Most Quenya words seem to be quite well-behaved, with no distinct 'stem' form to remember; you just add the ending and that's it." We will now 
attempt a fuller survey of the exceptional words that have special stem-forms.
\par 
\par Tolkien himself referred to these stem-forms in various ways. Sometimes he listed them as we do here, with a hyphen where the ending is to follow, e.g. }{\b\lang1033 n\'e9n}{\lang1033 , }{\b\lang1033 nen}{\lang1033 - "water" (Etym, entry }{\i\lang1033 NEN
}{\lang1033 ). This indicates that, say, the plural "waters" is to be }{\b\lang1033 neni}{\lang1033  rather than **}{\b\lang1033 n\'e9ni}{\lang1033 
. At other times, Tolkien himself mentions a complete inflected form, most often the plural or the genitive (which in the }{\i\lang1033 Etymologies}{\lang1033  still has the ending -(}{\b\lang1033 e}{\lang1033 )}{\b\lang1033 n}{\lang1033  rather than -}{
\b\lang1033 o}{\lang1033 ; in LotR-style Quenya, these forms as such are presumably still valid but must be taken as }{\i\lang1033 dative}{\lang1033  forms instead). The fact that }{\b\lang1033 talan}{\lang1033  "floor" has the stem }{\b\lang1033 talam}{
\lang1033 - Tolkien indicates by citing the plural form }{\b\lang1033 talami}{\lang1033  (see Etym, entry }{\i\lang1033 TAL}{\lang1033 , }{\i\lang1033 TALAM}{\lang1033 ). In the same entry he also mentions the word }{\b\lang1033 t\'e1l}{\lang1033 
 "foot". Before endings this word appears as }{\b\lang1033 tal}{\lang1033 - with a short vowel, and to indicate this, Tolkien cited the "g.sg." (genitive singular, later evidently dative singular) form }{\b\lang1033 talen}{\lang1033 .
\par 
