\lang1033  as the accusative of }{\b\lang1033 cirya}{\lang1033 , the lengthened final vowels would presumably preserve the original quality of the vowel: It was only }{\i\lang1033 short}{\lang1033  original final -}{\i\lang1033 i}{\lang1033  and -}{
\i\lang1033 u}{\lang1033  that turned into -}{\b\lang1033 \'eb}{\lang1033  and -}{\b\lang1033 o}{\lang1033  in Quenya. Thus }{\b\lang1033 fin\'eb}{\lang1033  "hair" with stem }{\b\lang1033 fini}{\lang1033 - would presumably have the accusative form }{
\b\lang1033 fin\'ed}{\lang1033 , whereas }{\b\lang1033 s\'falo}{\lang1033  "goblet" with stem }{\b\lang1033 s\'falu}{\lang1033 - would have the accusative form }{\b\lang1033 s\'fal\'fa}{\lang1033 
. But in Quenya as spoken in Middle-earth, the distinction between nominative and accusative was abandoned; "it was adequately expressed by word order" (Plotz letter). Phonologically, the older accusatives }{\b\lang1033 fin\'ed}{\lang1033  and }{
\b\lang1033 s\'fal\'fa}{\lang1033  could have produced Exilic Quenya }{\b\lang1033 fini}{\lang1033  and }{\b\lang1033 s\'falu}{\lang1033 , still remaining distinct from nominative }{\b\lang1033 fin\'eb}{\lang1033 , }{\b\lang1033 s\'falo}{\lang1033  
\endash  but Tolkien appears to be telling us that the distinct accusative forms were abandoned altogether.
\par 
\par }{\i\lang1033 Variation of final vowels?
\par }{\lang1033 Yet another kind of stem variation is so poorly attested that we cannot be sure whether it is really meant to exist "internally", or merely reflects Tolkien's indecision as to the exact shape of a word. We have already mentioned how }{
\b\lang1033 Valarauc}{\b\ul\lang1033 o}{\lang1033  "Demon of Might" (WJ:415, Sindarin }{\i\lang1033 balrog}{\lang1033 ) has the plural form }{\b\lang1033 Valarauc}{\b\ul\lang1033 a}{\b\lang1033 r}{\lang1033 , if we are to believe the }{\i\lang1033 
Silmarillion}{\lang1033 . Why this shift from a final -}{\b\lang1033 o}{\lang1033  in the simplex form to -}{\b\lang1033 a}{\lang1033 - before the plural ending -}{\b\lang1033 r}{\lang1033 ? Would we see the same change before other endings, e.g. dative ?
}{\b\lang1033 Valarauc}{\b\ul\lang1033 a}{\b\lang1033 n}{\lang1033  = "for a Valarauc}{\ul\lang1033 o}{\lang1033 "? We have only one other possible example of this variation of -}{\b\lang1033 o}{\lang1033  vs. -}{\b\lang1033 a}{\lang1033 -: In the }{
\i\lang1033 Etymologies}{\lang1033 , the Quenya word for "root" is }{\b\lang1033 sundo}{\lang1033  (entry }{\i\lang1033 SUD}{\lang1033 ), and this word also occurs elsewhere with reference to }{\i\lang1033 linguistic}{\lang1033 
 "roots" or bases (WJ:319). Yet the plural form "roots" appears to be }{\b\lang1033 sund}{\b\ul\lang1033 a}{\b\lang1033 r}{\lang1033  in the compound }{\b\lang1033 Tarmasundar}{\lang1033 
 "Roots of the Pillar" (the name of the slopes of Mt. Meneltarma in N\'famenor, UT:166). But such variation of -}{\b\lang1033 o}{\lang1033  vs. -}{\b\lang1033 a}{\lang1033 - seems difficult to justify within Tolkien's scenario for how Qu
enya had evolved from primitive Elvish. (By contrast, it is easily explained why final -}{\b\lang1033 o}{\lang1033  and -}{\b\lang1033 \'eb}{\lang1033  sometimes turn into -}{\b\lang1033 u}{\lang1033 - and -}{\b\lang1033 i}{\lang1033 
- before endings: original short -}{\i\lang1033 u}{\lang1033  and -}{\i\lang1033 i}{\lang1033  were changed in final position, but not elsewhere.) It should be noted that in neither
 of the two "attested" cases are the singular and the plural form provided }{\i\lang1033 in the same source}{\lang1033 , or even known to be closely contemporaneous. So maybe Tolkien sometimes thought of the Quenya word for "Balrog" as }{\b\lang1033 
Valarauca}{\lang1033  (hence pl. }{\b\lang1033 Valaraucar}{\lang1033 ) rather than }{\b\lang1033 Valarauco}{\lang1033 , and maybe he likewise wavered between }{\b\lang1033 sundo}{\lang1033  and }{\b\lang1033 sunda}{\lang1033 
 as the word for "root". It is also possible that }{\b\lang1033 sundo}{\lang1033  came to be used especially with reference to a linguistic "root" (base, root-word), whereas }{\b\lang1033 sunda}{\lang1033  refers to a natural "root" (of trees et
c., also used metaphorically of the slopes of a mountain).
\par 
\par }{\i\lang1033 Lost final vowels preserved before (consonant) endings?}{\i\f1 
\par }{\lang1033 When an ending beginning in a consonant (such as -}{\b\lang1033 nna}{\lang1033  for allative) is to be added to a noun ending in a consonant, a }{\i\lang1033 connecting vowel}{\lang1033  is sl
ipped in before the ending to avoid an impossible consonant cluster (unless the ending itself is somehow simplified). One may use -}{\b\lang1033 e}{\lang1033 
- as such a connecting vowel, this being the most neutral vowel (as in the attested allative form }{\b\lang1033 Elendil}{\b\ul\lang1033 e}{\b\lang1033 nna}{\lang1033  "to Elendil", 
PM:401). Quenya lost certain short final vowel as the language evolved from primitive Elvish, and one could speculate whether these vowels would be }{\i\lang1033 preserved}{\lang1033  before certain endings.
\par }\pard \fi708\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang1033 As noted above, the name }{\b\lang1033 Mandos}{\lang1033  has the stem }{\b\lang1033 Mandost}{\lang1033 - because the final element is a reduced form of }{\b\lang1033 osto}{\lang1033 
 "castle, fortress; city" (MR:350). Should the original final -}{\b\lang1033 o}{\lang1033  of }{\b\lang1033 osto}{\lang1033  be preserved in some case forms, e.g. ?}{\b\lang1033 Mandost}{\b\ul\lang1033 o}{\b\lang1033 nna}{\lang1033 
 as the allative "to Mandos"? Or would the neutral connecting vowel -}{\b\lang1033 e}{\lang1033 - be introduced here as well, so that we would have ?}{\b\lang1033 Mandost}{\b\ul\lang1033 e}{\b\lang1033 nna}{\lang1033  instead? What about a noun like }{
\b\lang1033 tol}{\lang1033  "island"? It is said to be derived from primitive }{\i\lang1033 tollo}{\lang1033  and therefore has the stem }{\b\lang1033 toll}{\lang1033 - as in the plural form }{\b\lang1033 tolli}{\lang1033  (typo "tolle" in Etym, entry }{
\i\lang1033 TOL}{\i\lang1033\super 2}{\lang1033 ). But would the lost final -}{\i\lang1033 o}{\lang1033  of the original }{\i\lang1033 tollo}{\lang1033  be preserved in some cases, e.g. locative ?}{\b\lang1033 tolloss\'eb}{\lang1033 
 "on an island"? If so, the stem of }{\b\lang1033 tol}{\lang1033  would have to be cited as }{\b\lang1033 toll}{\lang1033 (}{\b\lang1033 o}{\lang1033 )-, the final }{\b\lang1033 o}{\lang1033  turning up only before endings beginning in a consonant.
\par The instrumental form of }{\b\lang1033 ambar}{\lang1033 , }{\b\lang1033 ambart}{\lang1033 - "doom, fate" (more commonly }{\b\lang1033 umbar}{\lang1033 , }{\b\lang1033 umbart}{\lang1033 -) is attested as }{\b\lang1033 ambartanen}{\lang1033 
 in UT:138. Notice the vowel -}{\b\lang1033 a}{\lang1033 - turning up before the instrumental ending -}{\b\lang1033 nen}{\lang1033 . Tolkien's idea may be that the word for "fate" was something like }{\i\lang1033 m'bart}{\i\ul\lang1033 a}{\lang1033 
 in primitive Elvish, and that the original final -}{\i\lang1033 a}{\lang1033  is preserved in the instrumental form }{\b\lang1033 ambart}{\b\ul\lang1033 a}{\b\lang1033 nen}{\lang1033 , though this vowel (as well as the -}{\b\lang1033 t}{\lang1033 
 preceding it) has been lost in the simplex form }{\b\lang1033 ambar}{\lang1033  "doom".
\par The locative form of }{\b\lang1033 t\'e1l}{\lang1033 , }{\b\lang1033 tal}{\lang1033 - "foot" is said to appear as }{\b\lang1033 tal}{\b\ul\lang1033 a}{\b\lang1033 ss\'eb}{\lang1033  in one late manuscript (VT43:16; I assume that the }{\b\lang1033 t\'e1l}{
\lang1033  here discussed means "foot", though no gloss is provided in this source). Again the idea may be that "foot" was }{\i\lang1033 tala}{\lang1033  in primitive Elvish, and that the original final -}{\b\lang1033 a}{\lang1033 
 is preserved before endings beginning in a consonant. Yet the material is \endash  as usual \endash  less than consistent. In the }{\i\lang1033 Etymologies}{\lang1033 , entry }{\i\lang1033 TAL}{\lang1033 , the genitive (later presumably dative) form of }
{\b\lang1033 t\'e1l}{\lang1033  is given as }{\b\lang1033 talen}{\lang1033 , which form is probably best interpreted as }{\b\lang1033 tal}{\lang1033 - plus the genitive (later: dative) ending -}{\b\lang1033 n}{\lang1033 , the normal connecting vowel -}{
\b\lang1033 e}{\lang1033 - materializing between the noun and the ending to avoid the impossible form **}{\b\lang1033 taln}{\lang1033 . Would Tolkien later have said that the dative form of }{\b\lang1033 t\'e1l}{\lang1033 , }{\b\lang1033 tal}{\lang1033 
- should be }{\b\lang1033 tal}{\b\ul\lang1033 a}{\b\lang1033 n}{\lang1033  rather than }{\b\lang1033 tal}{\b\ul\lang1033 e}{\b\lang1033 n}{\lang1033 , to go with }{\b\lang1033 tal}{\b\ul\lang1033 a}{\b\lang1033 ss\'eb}{\lang1033 
 as the locative form? The form }{\b\lang1033 talass\'eb}{\lang1033  is supposedly taken from a fuller declension of }{\b\lang1033 t\'e1l}{\lang1033 , so the same (ca. 1967) source document may provide the answer \endash  but it is not available to us.

\par No definite conclusions can be reached at this stage; I would normally use the "neutral" connecting vowel -}{\b\lang1033 e}{\lang1033 - unless there is strong reason to believe that another vowel is to be preferred. It should be noted that -}{\b\lang1033 
i}{\lang1033  (rather than -}{\b\lang1033 r}{\lang1033 ) is in any case the preferred plural ending, so even if }{\b\lang1033 tol}{\lang1033 , }{\b\lang1033 toll}{\lang1033 - "island" does appear as }{\b\lang1033 tollo}{\lang1033 
- before endings beginning in a consonant, the plural "islands" should be }{\b\lang1033 tolli}{\lang1033  (attested in LT1:85) rather than **}{\b\lang1033 tollor}{\lang1033 
. The same principle would apply to all case endings incorporating the plural element -}{\b\lang1033 i}{\lang1033 -, like the plural dative -}{\b\lang1033 in}{\lang1033  or the plural instrumental -}{\b\lang1033 inen}{\lang1033 , and it woul
d also apply to the other case ending beginning in a vowel, the genitive in -}{\b\lang1033 o}{\lang1033 . So even though "by doom, by fate" is attested as }{\b\lang1033 ambart}{\b\ul\lang1033 a}{\b\lang1033 nen}{\lang1033 
, a corresponding plural form "by (the) fates" should probably be }{\b\lang1033 ambart}{\b\ul\lang1033 i}{\b\lang1033 nen}{\lang1033  with no -}{\b\lang1033 a}{\lang1033 - before the ending. The genitive would have to be }{\b\lang1033 ambarto}{\lang1033 
 (for older }{\i\lang1033 ambartao}{\lang1033  would be so altered even if it once did exist).
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang1033 
\par }{\cf2\lang1033 WORDS WE DON'T QUITE KNOW HOW TO INFLECT}{\lang1033 
\par There are certain Quenya nouns and verbs that by their shape are difficult to inflect in the various grammatical forms. Applying the normal rules may r
esult in slightly weird, or even phonologically impossible, forms. We will survey some of the problematic groups of words.
\par 
\par }{\i\lang1033 NOUNS}{\lang1033 
\par As noted above, there are some uncertainties relating to the behaviour of nouns in -}{\b\lang1033 il}{\lang1033  derived from primitive -}{\i\lang1033 la}{\lang1033 . Here follow certain other categories of "strange" nouns.
\par 
\par }{\i\lang1033 1. Monosyllabic nouns with a long vowel.}{\lang1033  The vowel of these words must presumably be modified in many of the inflected words. We will list the words involved.
\par 
\par With the vowel }{\i\lang1033 \'e1}{\lang1033 : }{\b\lang1033 m\'e1}{\lang1033  "hand" and }{\b\lang1033 r\'e1}{\lang1033  "lion". Some more occur in early "Qenya", e.g. }{\b\lang1033 c\'e1}{\lang1033  "jaw", }{\b\lang1033 r\'e1}{\lang1033  "arm", }{
\b\lang1033 s\'e1}{\lang1033  "fire", }{\b\lang1033 w\'e1}{\lang1033  "wind" (but other, polysyllabic words for these concepts are attested in Tolkien's later Quenya). Other "Qenya" words of this kind include }{\b\lang1033 \'e1}{\lang1033 
 "mind, inner thought", }{\b\lang1033 c\'e1}{\lang1033  "deed", }{\b\lang1033 f\'e1}{\lang1033  "lower airs", }{\b\lang1033 l\'e1}{\lang1033  "open space, moor" and }{\b\lang1033 qu\'e1}{\lang1033  "duck" (but }{\b\lang1033 \'e1}{\lang1033  and }{
\b\lang1033 l\'e1}{\lang1033  have other meanings in later Quenya).
\par 
\par With the vowel }{\i\lang1033 \'e9}{\lang1033 : }{\b\lang1033 p\'e9}{\lang1033  "lip" (so in a late source published in VT39:9; in the }{\i\lang1033 Etymologies}{\lang1033  the same word is glossed "mouth"), }{\b\lang1033 r\'e9}{\lang1033 
 "day" (a full cycle of 24 hours; "day" meaning the hours of daylight is }{\b\lang1033 aur\'eb}{\lang1033 ). In early "Qenya" we also have}{\b\lang1033  f\'e9}{\lang1033  "last hour, death", }{\b\lang1033 ny\'e9}{\lang1033  "a bleat", }{\b\lang1033 s\'e9}
{\lang1033  "eyeball", }{\b\lang1033 ty\'e9}{\lang1033  "tea", }{\b\lang1033 V\'e9}{\lang1033  name of a Vala.
\par 
\par With the vowel }{\i\lang1033 \'ed}{\lang1033 : }{\b\lang1033 n\'ed}{\lang1033  "woman; a female" (stated to be a poetic word; compare the more regular word }{\b\lang1033 n\'eds}{\lang1033 , }{\b\lang1033 niss}{\lang1033 -), }{\b\lang1033 p\'ed}{\lang1033 
 "a fly, small insect" (but "speck, spot" in early "Qenya").
\par 
\par With the vowel }{\i\lang1033 \'f3}{\lang1033 : }{\b\lang1033 l\'f3}{\lang1033  "night, a night", }{\b\lang1033 n\'f3}{\lang1033  "conception (= idea)", }{\b\lang1033 t\'f3}{\lang1033  "wool". In "Qenya" we also have forms like }{\b\lang1033 v\'f3}{
\lang1033 , }{\b\lang1033 y\'f3}{\lang1033 , both meaning "son" (but these forms Tolkien apparently replaced by }{\b\lang1033 yondo}{\lang1033  later). The "Qenya" form }{\b\lang1033 \'d3}{\lang1033 , a poetic wo
rd for the sea, may also be mentioned, as may }{\b\lang1033 h\'f3}{\lang1033  "owl".
\par 
\par With the vowel }{\i\lang1033 \'fa}{\lang1033 : }{\b\lang1033 c\'fa}{\lang1033  "arch, crescent" (in early "Qenya" also "crescent moon"), }{\b\lang1033 l\'fa}{\lang1033  "a time, occasion", }{\b\lang1033 R\'fa}{\lang1033  "Dr\'fb
g, member of a certain Mannish race of Middle-earth" (see UT:385), }{\b\lang1033 s\'fa}{\lang1033  "noise of wind". The latter also occurs in early "Qenya", which language also has }{\b\lang1033 r\'fa}{\lang1033 
 "steadfastness" (hardly a valid word in LotR-style Quenya).}{\b\lang1033 
\par }{\lang1033 
\par One question without any entirely clear answer is how to form the nominative plural of these words. Normally, words ending in a vowel (except -}{\b\lang1033 \'eb}{\lang1033 ) add the ending -}{\b\lang1033 r}{\lang1033 
 in the plural. Does this also apply to words like these? If }{\b\lang1033 c\'fa}{\lang1033  is "crescent", should "crescents" be }{\b\lang1033 c\'far}{\lang1033 ?
\par 
\par Some information about the word }{\b\lang1033 m\'e1}{\lang1033  "hand" surfaced in VT47:6. Tolkien stated that it never received the plural ending -}{\b\lang1033 r}{\lang1033 , in part because this would make the word clash with the noun }{\b\lang1033 m
\'e1r}{\lang1033  "dwelling" (instead, only the plural ending -}{\b\lang1033 li}{\lang1033  was used: }{\b\lang1033 m\'e1li}{\lang1033  "hands" \endash  though otherwise, the ending -}{\b\lang1033 li}{\lang1033 
 is associated with the special "partitive plural" discussed above). Since the form **}{\b\lang1033 m\'e1r}{\lang1033  was not used for such a specific reason (the clash with }{\b\lang1033 m\'e1r}{\lang1033  "dwelling"), this }{\i\lang1033 may}{\lang1033 
 indirectly suggest that most of these nouns can indeed have plural forms in -}{\b\lang1033 r}{\lang1033 .
\par 
\par What about the nouns in -}{\b\lang1033 \'e9}{\lang1033 ? We know that }{\i\lang1033 polysyllabic}{\lang1033  nouns in -}{\b\lang1033 \'eb}{\lang1033  normally form their plurals in -}{\b\lang1033 i}{\lang1033 . Should the plural form of }{\b\lang1033 r
\'e9}{\lang1033  "day" be }{\b\lang1033 r\'ed}{\lang1033 ? Or maybe }{\b\lang1033 r\'e9r}{\lang1033 ? For what it is worth: When the word }{\b\lang1033 r\'e9}{\lang1033  "day" appears at the end of a compound, it is shortened to -}{\b\lang1033 r\'eb}{
\lang1033 , with a plural form in -}{\b\lang1033 ri}{\lang1033  (compare }{\b\lang1033 Yesta}{\b\ul\lang1033 r\'eb}{\lang1033 , New Year's Day in the Elvish calendar, with }{\b\lang1033 ende}{\b\ul\lang1033 ri}{\lang1033 
 "middle-days", days inserted between certain months). But this may not necessarily support ?}{\b\lang1033 r\'ed}{\lang1033  as the plural form "days" when the word occurs by itself. At the end of compounds, words that normally have plural forms in -}{
\b\lang1033 r}{\lang1033  may be transferred to the }{\b\lang1033 i}{\lang1033 -plurals instead. For instance, the prominent }{\b\lang1033 r}{\lang1033 -plural }{\b\lang1033 Noldor}{\lang1033  (archaic }{\b\lang1033 Ngoldor}{\lang1033 ) may be contra
sted with -}{\b\lang1033 ngoldi}{\lang1033  in the compound }{\b\lang1033 Etyangoldi}{\lang1033  "Exiled Noldor" (WJ:374; the singular is probably }{\b\lang1033 Etyangol}{\lang1033  with stem }{\b\lang1033 Etyangold}{\lang1033 
-). Words may be shortened and reduced at the end of compounds, like }{\b\lang1033 r\'e9}{\lang1033  loses its long vowel in the compound form -}{\b\lang1033 r\'eb}{\lang1033 , and this may also have consequences for the way the word is inflected.
\par 
\par Not all of these words behave in such a way, however. In the case of the }{\b\lang1033 n\'f3}{\lang1033  "conception" (idea), Tolkien indicated that this word has the stem-form }{\b\lang1033 n\'f3w}{\lang1033 - (he listed the pl. }{\b\lang1033 n\'f3wi}{
\lang1033 ), preserving the second consonant of the original root }{\i\lang1033 NOWO}{\lang1033  listed in the }{\i\lang1033 Etymologies}{\lang1033 . The word }{\b\lang1033 t\'f3}{\lang1033  "wool", derived from a root }{\i\lang1033 TOW}{\lang1033 
, may likewise have the stem }{\b\lang1033 t\'f3w}{\lang1033 -. Before the genitive ending -}{\b\lang1033 o}{\lang1033 , this }{\b\lang1033 w}{\lang1033  would likely change to }{\b\lang1033 v}{\lang1033 , since }{\b\lang1033 wo}{\lang1033 
 is apparently not allowed in Quenya. If so, the genitive of }{\b\lang1033 n\'f3}{\lang1033  and }{\b\lang1033 t\'f3}{\lang1033  would be }{\b\lang1033 n\'f3vo}{\lang1033 , }{\b\lang1033 t\'f3vo}{\lang1033 . The word }{\b\lang1033 r\'e1}{\lang1033 
 "lion" has the stem-form }{\b\lang1033 r\'e1v}{\lang1033 -, hence pl. }{\b\lang1033 r\'e1vi}{\lang1033  (listed by Tolkien) and genitive presumably }{\b\lang1033 r\'e1vo}{\lang1033 .
\par 
\par But it is not always clear how the genitive ending -}{\b\lang1033 o}{\lang1033  would be added to these words. Adding the genitive ending -}{\b\lang1033 o}{\lang1033  to a word like }{\b\lang1033 l\'f3}{\lang1033  "night" would presumably not alter the wo
rd in any way; the -}{\b\lang1033 o}{\lang1033  simply merges into the already-long }{\b\lang1033 \'f3}{\lang1033 , and only the context would indicate that the noun is to be understood as a genitive. But what about the words in -}{\b\lang1033 \'e1}{
\lang1033 ? We know that the genitive ending -}{\b\lang1033 o}{\lang1033  normally displaces a final -}{\b\lang1033 a}{\lang1033 , as in }{\b\lang1033 Vardo}{\lang1033  "Varda's" (}{\i\lang1033 Nam\'e1ri\'eb}{\lang1033 ). Should the genitive form of }{
\b\lang1033 m\'e1}{\lang1033  "hand" be }{\b\lang1033 m\'f3}{\lang1033 ? Or could it simply be }{\b\lang1033 m\'e1o}{\lang1033 ? The combination }{\i\lang1033 ao}{\lang1033  is apparently not allowed in Quenya; does it make any difference if the }{
\b\lang1033 \'e1}{\lang1033  is long? \endash  In the case of monosyllabic nouns ending in other vowels than -}{\b\lang1033 \'e1}{\lang1033  and -}{\b\lang1033 \'f3}{\lang1033 , we must probably assume that the genitive ending is simply added to the word.

\par 
\par The possessive ending -}{\b\lang1033 va}{\lang1033  would also be added directly, presumably merging with -}{\b\lang1033 v}{\lang1033  and -}{\b\lang1033 w}{\lang1033  in the case of the nouns with stem-forms in these consonants. "Of a lion
" should evidently be }{\b\lang1033 r\'e1va}{\lang1033 ; one may wonder whether }{\b\lang1033 n\'f3wa}{\lang1033  or }{\b\lang1033 n\'f3va}{\lang1033  is the best option as the possessive form of }{\b\lang1033 n\'f3}{\lang1033 , }{\b\lang1033 n\'f3w}{
\lang1033 - "conception".
\par 
\par Before endings with a double consonant or a consonant cluster, the long vowel of these nouns must be shortened. Thus the plural ablative of }{\b\lang1033 m\'e1}{\lang1033  "hand" is attested in LR:72 as }{\b\lang1033 mannar}{\lang1033 
 (since "into hands" could not be the impossible form }{\b\lang1033 m\'e1nnar}{\lang1033 ). We must assume it would be the same with pronominal endings, e.g. }{\b\lang1033 lulma}{\lang1033  "our time" from }{\b\lang1033 l\'fa}{\lang1033  "time" (hardly }{
\b\lang1033 l\'falma}{\lang1033 ). However, the example }{\b\lang1033 m\'e1ryat}{\lang1033  "her hands" from }{\i\lang1033 Nam\'e1ri\'eb}{\lang1033  indicates that the long vowels of these words }{\i\lang1033 do}{\lang1033 
 remain long before pronominal endings including combinations in -}{\b\lang1033 y}{\lang1033 -, such as -}{\b\lang1033 nya}{\lang1033  "my", -}{\b\lang1033 lya}{\lang1033  "thy" and -}{\b\lang1033 rya}{\lang1033 
 "his/her". As we discussed in Lesson Fifteen, it is not entirely clear how we are to analyze groups like }{\b\lang1033 ny}{\lang1033 , }{\b\lang1033 ly}{\lang1033 , }{\b\lang1033 ry}{\lang1033 : are they }{\i\lang1033 single}{\lang1033 
 palatalized consonants, so that a long vowel can survive in front of them? But they seem to count as consonant clusters, or long consonants, for the purpose of determining which syllable receives the stress.
\par 
\par 2. }{\i\lang1033 Nouns in -}{\b\i\lang1033 i\'eb}{\i\lang1033  and -}{\b\i\lang1033 i}{\i\lang1033 : }{\lang1033 There are also some unanswered questions relating to nouns in -}{\b\lang1033 i\'eb}{\lang1033  and -}{\b\lang1033 i}{\lang1033 , like }{
\b\lang1033 li\'eb}{\lang1033  "people", }{\b\lang1033 arani\'eb}{\lang1033  "kingdom" or }{\b\lang1033 t\'e1ri}{\lang1033  "queen". How is the genitive ending -}{\b\lang1033 o}{\lang1033  to be added to nouns in -}{\b\lang1033 i\'eb}{\lang1033 
? There would be three vowels in sequence, and words like }{\b\lang1033 li\'ebo}{\lang1033 , }{\b\lang1033 arani\'ebo}{\lang1033  would have to be accented on the }{\b\lang1033 i}{\lang1033 , sounding rather awkward. Some believe the }{\b\lang1033 \'eb}{
\lang1033  would drop out, producing forms like }{\b\lang1033 lio}{\lang1033  or }{\b\lang1033 aranio}{\lang1033 , but this cannot be proved. The present writer is inclined to think that the }{\b\lang1033 e}{\lang1033  would be lengthened to }{
\b\lang1033 \'e9}{\lang1033  so as to attract the stress: }{\b\lang1033 li\'e9o}{\lang1033 , }{\b\lang1033 arani\'e9o}{\lang1033 .
\par 
\par What about the case endings already including the vowel -}{\b\lang1033 i}{\lang1033 , notably -}{\b\lang1033 in}{\lang1033  for plural dative, -}{\b\lang1033 iva}{\lang1033  for plural possessive and -}{\b\lang1033 inen}{\lang1033 
 for plural instrumental? (There is also the "mystery case" from the Plotz Letter, probably a short locative, that has the ending -}{\b\lang1033 is}{\lang1033  in the plural.) As far as we know, the plural dative of }{\b\lang1033 t\'e1ri}{\lang1033 
 "queen" must be formed by combining }{\b\lang1033 t\'e1ri}{\lang1033  and -}{\b\lang1033 in}{\lang1033 , but what does this result in? Do we see }{\b\lang1033 t\'e1r\'edn}{\lang1033  with a long }{\b\lang1033 \'ed}{\lang1033 , for }{\i\lang1033 t\'e1
ri-in}{\lang1033 ? In the Plotz Letter, Tolkien states that in words of two or more syllables, a long vowel was shortened before a final consonant. If so, older }{\b\lang1033 t\'e1r\'edn}{\lang1033  would become }{\b\lang1033 t\'e1rin}{\lang1033 
, identical to the }{\i\lang1033 singular}{\lang1033  form of the dative (}{\b\lang1033 t\'e1ri}{\lang1033  + -}{\b\lang1033 n}{\lang1033 ). It may be that only the context can tell us whether }{\b\lang1033 t\'e1rin}{\lang1033 
 is singular "for a queen" or plural "for queens".
\par 
\par On the other hand, in the possessive case there may be a distinction between plural }{\b\lang1033 t\'e1r\'edva}{\lang1033  "queens', of queens" (}{\b\lang1033 t\'e1ri}{\lang1033  + -}{\b\lang1033 iva}{\lang1033 ) and singular }{\b\lang1033 t\'e1riva}{
\lang1033  "queen's, of a queen" (simply }{\b\lang1033 t\'e1ri}{\lang1033  + -}{\b\lang1033 va}{\lang1033 ). The forms would be stressed differently, making them clearly distinct.
\par 
\par Words in -}{\b\lang1033 i\'eb}{\lang1033  (mostly abstracts, gerunds and some feminines like }{\b\lang1033 Vali\'eb}{\lang1033 ) pose their own problems in this regard. What is the dative plural and possessive plural of, say, }{\b\lang1033 Vali\'eb}{
\lang1033 ? Adding the endings -}{\b\lang1033 in}{\lang1033  and -}{\b\lang1033 iva}{\lang1033  produces the impossible forms **}{\b\lang1033 Valiein}{\lang1033  and **}{\b\lang1033 Valieiva}{\lang1033 : Quenya does not possess the diphthong }{
\b\lang1033 ei}{\lang1033 . Where it formerly occurred it normally became }{\b\lang1033 \'ed}{\lang1033 , but according to a note by Tolkien published in VT48:7, it turned into }{\b\lang1033 \'e9}{\lang1033  following short }{\b\lang1033 i}{\lang1033 
. If so, impossible *}{\b\lang1033 Valiein}{\lang1033  first turns into }{\b\lang1033 Vali\'e9n}{\lang1033 
. Then, according to the Plotz rule just mentioned, a long vowel before a final consonant is shortened in polysyllabic words: Thus we presumably arrive at }{\b\lang1033 Valien}{\lang1033 . As in the case of }{\b\lang1033 t\'e1rin}{\lang1033 
, the distinction between singular and plural is lost, for }{\b\lang1033 Valien}{\lang1033  could just as well be the }{\i\lang1033 singular}{\lang1033  dative "for a Vali\'eb" (}{\b\lang1033 Vali\'eb}{\lang1033  + -}{\b\lang1033 n}{\lang1033 
). Similarly, plural possessive **}{\b\lang1033 Valieiva}{\lang1033  would turn into }{\b\lang1033 Vali\'e9va}{\lang1033 , which could also be singular instead (as if }{\b\lang1033 Vali\'eb}{\lang1033  + -}{\b\lang1033 va}{\lang1033 ). 
\par 
\par The instrumental forms would be similar to the possessive forms deduced above, only with -}{\b\lang1033 nen}{\lang1033  instead of -}{\b\lang1033 va}{\lang1033 .
\par 
\par It is almost tempting to start from the nominative plural }{\b\lang1033 Valier}{\lang1033  and use ?}{\b\lang1033 Vali\'e9rin}{\lang1033  as the dative plural (and perhaps ?}{\b\lang1033 Valierwa}{\lang1033  as the possessive plural!) But there is n
o trace of such forms in published Tolkien material.
\par 
\par 3. }{\i\lang1033 Nouns in -}{\b\i\lang1033 oa}{\lang1033 : Quenya possesses a number of nouns ending in the combination -}{\b\lang1033 oa}{\lang1033 , most notably }{\b\lang1033 coa}{\lang1033  "house", }{\b\lang1033 hroa}{\lang1033  "body", }{
\b\lang1033 loa}{\lang1033  "year" (literally "growth"), }{\b\lang1033 noa}{\lang1033  "conception" (idea), }{\b\lang1033 roa}{\lang1033  "dog", }{\b\lang1033 toa}{\lang1033  "wood". Most of these are meant to be descended from earlier forms in -}{
\i\lang1033 awa}{\lang1033  or -}{\i\lang1033 aw\'e2}{\lang1033  (see especially VT47:35), a combination that became -}{\b\lang1033 oa}{\lang1033  in Quenya.
\par 
\par In early "Qenya" we also have }{\b\lang1033 foa}{\lang1033  "hoard, treasure", }{\b\lang1033 loa}{\lang1033  "life" (probably obsoleted by }{\b\lang1033 loa}{\lang1033  "year, growth" in later Quenya), }{\b\lang1033 moa}{\lang1033  "sheep", }{\b\lang1033 
oa}{\lang1033  "wool", }{\b\lang1033 poa}{\lang1033  "beard" and }{\b\lang1033 roa}{\lang1033  "wild beast". }{\b\lang1033 Moa}{\lang1033 , }{\b\lang1033 oa}{\lang1033  and }{\b\lang1033 poa}{\lang1033  are perhaps obsoleted by }{\b\lang1033 m\'e1ma}{
\lang1033 , }{\b\lang1033 t\'f3}{\lang1033  and }{\b\lang1033 fanga}{\lang1033  of the same meaning in Tolkien's later Quenya; moreover, }{\b\lang1033 roa}{\lang1033  was redefined as "dog" (see above). Anyway, it seems that most of
 these were also meant to represent older forms in -}{\i\lang1033 awa}{\lang1033  or -}{\i\lang1033 ava}{\lang1033 , although }{\b\lang1033 foa}{\lang1033  and }{\b\lang1033 oa}{\lang1033  are derived from roots where the first vowel was }{\i\lang1033 o}{
\lang1033  from the beginning. (The same goes for the later form }{\b\lang1033 noa}{\lang1033  "conception", derived from a root }{\i\lang1033 NOWO}{\lang1033  in the }{\i\lang1033 Etymologies}{\lang1033 .)
\par 
\par The main "problem" relating to these forms is this: What happens if we add the genitive ending -}{\b\lang1033 o}{\lang1033 ? Since this ending displaces a final -}{\b\lang1033 a}{\lang1033 , what does a word like }{\b\lang1033 hroa}{\lang1033 
 "body" turn into in the genitive case? Is "body's" to be }{\b\lang1033 hr\'f3}{\lang1033 , for the impossible form }{\b\lang1033 hro'o}{\lang1033 ?!
\par 
\par If we are to take into account the supposed phonological development, we could reach pretty surprising conclusions. The word }{\b\lang1033 hroa}{\lang1033  is meant to be descended from primitive }{\i\lang1033 sraw\'e2}{\lang1033 , }{\i\lang1033 srawa}{
\lang1033  (MR:350, VT47:35). The genitive ending -}{\b\lang1033 o}{\lang1033  represents primitive -}{\i\lang1033 ho}{\lang1033 , merging into a final -}{\i\lang1033 a}{\lang1033  after the loss of }{\i\lang1033 h}{\lang1033 . If }{\i\lang1033 srawa-ho}{
\lang1033  became }{\i\lang1033 srawao}{\lang1033  and then }{\i\lang1033 sraw\'f4}{\lang1033  early enough, the Quenya outcome would be }{\b\lang1033 hravo}{\lang1033 ! Similarly, the genitive of }{\b\lang1033 coa}{\lang1033  "house" would be }{
\b\lang1033 cavo}{\lang1033 , the genitive of }{\b\lang1033 loa}{\lang1033  "year" would be }{\b\lang1033 lavo}{\lang1033 , and so on (reflecting ancient forms like }{\i\lang1033 kawa}{\lang1033  "house", }{\i\lang1033 lawa}{\lang1033 
 "growth, year": VT47:35, 42:10). 
\par 
\par However, the genitive of }{\b\lang1033 noa}{\lang1033  "conception" is presumably }{\b\lang1033 novo}{\lang1033 , since the root is }{\i\lang1033 NOWO}{\lang1033  rather than **}{\i\lang1033 NAWA}{\lang1033 . (Compare }{\b\lang1033 n\'f3wi}{\lang1033 
 as the plural form of }{\b\lang1033 n\'f3}{\lang1033 , a shorter synonym of }{\b\lang1033 noa}{\lang1033  derived from the same root. Before -}{\b\lang1033 o}{\lang1033 , we must assume that }{\b\lang1033 w}{\lang1033  becomes }{\b\lang1033 v}{\lang1033 
.)
\par 
\par }{\i\lang1033 VERBS
\par }{\lang1033 
\par In the case of a few verbs, it is difficult to predict the form of the }{\i\lang1033 gerund}{\lang1033  and the }{\i\lang1033 perfect}{\lang1033  tense. One such verb is }{\b\lang1033 feuya}{\lang1033 - "abhor", derived from the root }{\i\lang1033 PHEW}{
\lang1033  (see this entry in the }{\i\lang1033 Etymologies}{\lang1033 ). According to our general understanding, the gerundial ending -}{\b\lang1033 i\'eb}{\lang1033  displaces the ending -}{\b\lang1033 ya}{\lang1033 . But "}{\b\lang1033 feui\'eb}{
\lang1033 " would be a rather unlikely form as the gerund "abhorring". Perhaps we must refer back to the original root }{\i\lang1033 PHEW}{\lang1033 . In }{\b\lang1033 feuya}{\lang1033 -, the original }{\i\lang1033 w}{\lang1033  has changed to }{
\b\lang1033 u}{\lang1033  before another consonant: the }{\b\lang1033 y}{\lang1033  of the verbal ending -}{\b\lang1033 ya}{\lang1033 . But between vowels, the }{\i\lang1033 w}{\lang1033  of Primitive Elvish normally turns into Quenya }{\b\lang1033 v}{
\lang1033  (the only notable exception seems to be that }{\b\lang1033 w}{\lang1033  is preserved unchanged following the diphthong }{\b\lang1033 ai}{\lang1033 , as in }{\b\lang1033 aiw\'eb}{\lang1033  "bird"). So }{\i\lang1033 PHEW}{\lang1033 
 "abhor" + the gerundial ending -}{\b\lang1033 i\'eb}{\lang1033  would perhaps come out in Quenya as }{\b\lang1033 fevi\'eb}{\lang1033 . Similarly, the perfect "has abhorred" could conceivably be }{\b\lang1033 ef\'e9vi\'eb}{\lang1033 .
\par 
\par }{\b\lang1033 S\'faya}{\lang1033 - "to breathe" is even more problematic. Should the gerund be }{\b\lang1033 sui\'eb}{\lang1033 , the first vowel of the gerundial ending -}{\b\lang1033 i\'eb}{\lang1033  merging w
ith the stem-vowel to produce the diphthong }{\b\lang1033 ui}{\lang1033 ? And will }{\b\lang1033 usui\'eb}{\lang1033  do as the perfect "has breathed"?
\par 
\par Verbs in }{\b\lang1033 \'e9}{\lang1033  + }{\b\lang1033 ya}{\lang1033  would be particularly difficult. No such verb seems to be attested, but they probably could exist: In the }{\i\lang1033 Etymologies}{\lang1033  we have }{\i\lang1033 thio}{\lang1033 
 as the Noldorin (later: Sindarin) verb "to seem". The root is }{\i\lang1033 TH\'ca}{\lang1033 , so the primitive form is clearly meant to be }{\i\lang1033 th\'eay\'e2}{\lang1033 -; this would apparently come out as }{\b\lang1033 s\'e9ya}{\lang1033 
- in Quenya. Some post-Tolkien writers have already used this unattested verb. Granted that this extrapolation is correct, what would the gerund of such a verb be? Since Quenya does not have }{\b\lang1033 ei}{\lang1033 , we cannot go for **}{\b\lang1033 
sei\'eb}{\lang1033 . Historically, older }{\b\lang1033 ei}{\lang1033  normally becomes long }{\b\lang1033 \'ed}{\lang1033  in Quenya. }{\b\lang1033 S\'ed\'eb}{\lang1033 , then? Perfect }{\b\lang1033 es\'ed\'eb}{\lang1033 
, the augment reflecting the original stem-vowel of the root }{\i\lang1033 TH\'ca}{\lang1033 ?
\par 
\par The verb "to change" is apparently }{\b\lang1033 ahya}{\lang1033 - (only attested in the past tense: }{\b\lang1033 ahyan\'eb}{\lang1033 ). Should we go for }{\b\lang1033 ahi\'eb}{\lang1033  as the gerund "changing"? The problem is that }{\b\lang1033 hy}{
\lang1033  is not }{\b\lang1033 h}{\lang1033  + }{\b\lang1033 y}{\lang1033 , but a single unitary sound, like the German }{\i\lang1033 ich-Laut}{\lang1033  (of which the }{\i\lang1033 h}{\lang1033  of English }{\i\lang1033 huge}{\lang1033 , }{\i\lang1033 
human}{\lang1033  is a weak version). In }{\b\lang1033 ahi\'eb}{\lang1033  the }{\b\lang1033 h}{\lang1033  would be a different sound, like normal English breath-}{\b\lang1033 h}{\lang1033 . Should we use }{\b\lang1033 ahyi\'eb}{\lang1033 , then? Hardly;
 Quenya phonology does not seem to allow }{\b\lang1033 hy}{\lang1033  before the vowel }{\b\lang1033 i}{\lang1033 . It is a problem that we don't know what the ancestral form of }{\b\lang1033 ahya}{\lang1033 - is supposed to be: maybe }{\i\lang1033 akh-y
\'e2}{\lang1033 ? If the original root is }{\i\lang1033 AKH}{\lang1033 , one could argue that the gerund should indeed be }{\b\lang1033 ahi\'eb}{\lang1033 : Original }{\i\lang1033 kh}{\lang1033  becomes }{\b\lang1033 h}{\lang1033  between vowels, whereas 
}{\i\lang1033 kh}{\lang1033  + }{\i\lang1033 y}{\lang1033  would produce }{\b\lang1033 hy}{\lang1033 , as in }{\b\lang1033 ahya}{\lang1033 -. The perfect "has changed" could conceivably be }{\b\lang1033 ah\'e1hi\'eb}{\lang1033 
, if we are right to assume that the entire initial syllable is prefixed as an augment when the verb begins in a vowel: primitive }{\i\lang1033 akh-\'e2khii\'ea}{\lang1033 . But there are also those who would argue that a form like }{\b\lang1033 ahy\'e1ni
\'eb}{\lang1033  may be used, based on the past tense }{\b\lang1033 ahyan\'eb}{\lang1033 . Tolkien indicated that "intrusion of }{\i\lang1033 n}{\lang1033  from the past [tense]" into the perfect tense sometimes occurred in the language (WJ:366).
\par 
\par Early "Qenya" has verbs in -}{\b\lang1033 itya}{\lang1033 , like }{\b\lang1033 naitya}{\lang1033 - "to shame, abuse" and }{\b\lang1033 paitya}{\lang1033 - "to repay". If it is at all possible
 to fit them into Tolkien's later scheme, we must assume that the gerundial ending -}{\b\lang1033 i\'eb}{\lang1033  replaces -}{\b\lang1033 ya}{\lang1033  (so that we would have }{\b\lang1033 naiti\'eb}{\lang1033  "shaming", }{\b\lang1033 paiti\'eb}{
\lang1033  "repaying"), and then maybe }{\b\lang1033 anaiti\'eb}{\lang1033 , }{\b\lang1033 apaiti\'eb}{\lang1033  in the perfect tense.
\par 
\par The verb }{\b\lang1033 tuia}{\lang1033 - "to spring, sprout" is apparently derived from the root }{\i\lang1033 TUY}{\lang1033  with the simple ending -}{\b\lang1033 a}{\lang1033 , early }{\i\lang1033 tuya}{\lang1033 - becoming }{\b\lang1033 tuia}{
\lang1033 - in later Quenya. Maybe we could have the gerund }{\b\lang1033 tui\'eb}{\lang1033 , for impossible }{\i\lang1033 tuyi\'eb}{\lang1033 , and the perfect }{\b\lang1033 utui\'eb}{\lang1033 , for }{\i\lang1033 ut\'fayi\'eb}{\lang1033 ?!
\par 
\par A verb like }{\b\lang1033 lia}{\lang1033 - "twine" (from early "Qenya") is very difficult to adopt into Tolkien's later inflection scheme. It is said to be derived from }{\i\lang1033 liya}{\lang1033  (QL:53). A gerund }{\b\lang1033 li\'eb}{\lang1033 
 (for impossible }{\i\lang1033 liyi\'eb}{\lang1033 ) would clash with }{\b\lang1033 li\'eb}{\lang1033  "people", a noun that was present already in Tolkien's early development of Qenya/Quenya (it is indeed listed on the same page in the QL as the verb }{
\b\lang1033 lia}{\lang1033 -). The perfect, if it is at all possible to construct one, would have to be something like }{\b\lang1033 il\'ed\'eb}{\lang1033  (for }{\i\lang1033 il\'edyi\'eb}{\lang1033 ).
\par 
\par We can hardly avoid the problems encountered when we try to construct the }{\i\lang1033 perfect}{\lang1033  tense forms of such cumbersome verbs, but the }{\i\lang1033 gerund}{\lang1033  in -}{\b\lang1033 i\'eb}{\lang1033 
 may perhaps be replaced by some other abstract ending; the best alternative is probably -}{\b\lang1033 l\'eb}{\lang1033 . So from verbs like }{\b\lang1033 ahya}{\lang1033 - "change", }{\b\lang1033 s\'faya}{\lang1033 - "breathe" and }{\b\lang1033 lia}{
\lang1033 - "twine", we could derive abstract nouns like }{\b\lang1033 ahyal\'eb}{\lang1033  "changing, change", }{\b\lang1033 s\'fayal\'eb}{\lang1033  "breathing" and }{\b\lang1033 lial\'eb}{\lang1033  "twining".
\par }}